    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside trio in the outfield, headlined by **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03), who earns our highest efficiency score of the day. His matchup against Abbott is a prime environment for production, bolstered by an excellent 19% boost from the starting pitcher\u2019s skill profile and a favorable platoon advantage. He is joined by **Mickey Moniak** (69.68) and **Corbin Carroll** (66.61) as our primary drivers for SB and run production. **Mickey Moniak** is particularly intriguing; while the park factor is slightly negative, the combination of a +14% platoon edge and the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery\u2014which plays directly into our strategy of overweighting speed\u2014makes him a elite-tier start. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) also offers a massive ceiling today, as his power profile benefits significantly from park factors and a favorable 4.62 xERA from Baz, positioning him as our most reliable run-producer.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Josh Naylor**, who finished just outside the starting nine with a 44.64 score. While his base projection is robust, he was ultimately pushed out by the efficiency of our mid-tier infielders and the high-leverage scoring associated with **Travis Bazzana** (61.46) at the top of the order. **Travis Bazzana** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost and the \"leadoff effect,\" giving him more opportunities to capitalize on the SB scarcity we prioritize. Conversely, we are steering clear of **Edwin Arroyo** (40.01) despite his inclusion in the lineup, as he sits right on our 40-point floor; he remains the weakest link in our daily configuration, salvaged only by his switch-hitting flexibility. We are actively monitoring the environment for **Austin Riley** (48.51), whose score is suppressed by a difficult matchup against May and a lower-than-average SB threat from the opposition, making him a marginal play we will be tracking closely for defensive replacement or mid-game adjustments.",
